=== plain-80: width 80, height 24, profile notty, dark, glyphs ascii, plain
# play backup/relative -----------------------------------------$
$
# Note$
# Play mode: hardn's own screens and prompts, on the real$
# plan/apply engine against a simulated host, hostfake's$
# recording host.System, whose commands a model of the host$
# answers. Nothing on this machine changes: every command and$
# file is the fake's, and its record is shown when play ends.$
$
# Info: backup/relative$
# web-01.example.net runs Debian 13. hardn.yml says backupPath:$
# backups, a relative path, as origin/main's Backup screen saves$
# one.$
$
# Backup Settings ##############################################$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup State         Enabled$
  [ ] Backup Path          backups Not absolute$
$
  Select an option$
$
   1) Backup State         Turn backups off$
   2) Backup Path          Change backup path$
   3) Verify Directory     Check the path, writing nothing$
   4) Backup Runs$
$
   0) Return$
$
    > Enter selection [0-4 or q]: 3$
$
# Warning$
# "backups" is not an absolute path. Start it with /.$
$
  Nothing was written.$
$
# Backup Settings ##############################################$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup State         Enabled$
  [ ] Backup Path          backups Not absolute$
$
  Select an option$
$
   1) Backup State         Turn backups off$
   2) Backup Path          Change backup path$
   3) Verify Directory     Check the path, writing nothing$
   4) Backup Runs$
$
   0) Return$
$
    > Enter selection [0-4 or q]: 1$
$
# Warning$
# "backups" is not an absolute path. Start it with /.$
$
# Backup Settings ##############################################$
$
# Backup Configuration -----------------------------------------$
$
  [+] Backup State         Enabled$
  [ ] Backup Path          backups Not absolute$
$
  Select an option$
$
   1) Backup State         Turn backups off$
   2) Backup Path          Change backup path$
   3) Verify Directory     Check the path, writing nothing$
   4) Backup Runs$
$
   0) Return$
$
    > Enter selection [0-4 or q]: 0$
$
# simulated system ---------------------------------------------$
$
# Note$
# Nothing was applied.$
$

